Let $E$ denote the given expression. Apply $\sin x\cos x=\frac{1}{2}\sin(2x)$ at $x=\frac{\pi}{16}$. Dividing by secant multiplies by cosine because $1/\sec x=\cos x$. The resulting pair at $x=\frac{\pi}{8}$ permits a second application.
$$
\begin{aligned}
E&=\frac{\sin\left(\frac{\pi}{8}\right)}{\sec\left(\frac{\pi}{8}\right)}\\&=\sin\left(\frac{\pi}{8}\right)\cos\left(\frac{\pi}{8}\right)\\&=\frac{1}{2}\,\sin\left(\frac{\pi}{4}\right)
\end{aligned}
$$
The terminal sine is $\sin\left(\frac{\pi}{4}\right)=\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}$.
Therefore,
$$
\begin{aligned}
E&=\frac{1}{2}\,\left(\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}\right)\\&=\frac{\sqrt{2}}{4}.
\end{aligned}
$$

Let $E$ denote the given expression. Apply $\sin x\cos x=\frac{1}{2}\sin(2x)$ at $x=7.5^\circ$ in the denominator. Use $\sec x=1/\cos x$, then apply the double-angle identity again at $x=15^\circ$.
$$
\begin{aligned}
E&=\frac{4\,\sec\left(15^\circ\right)}{\sin\left(15^\circ\right)}\\&=\frac{4}{\sin\left(15^\circ\right)\cos\left(15^\circ\right)}\\&=\frac{8}{\sin\left(30^\circ\right)}
\end{aligned}
$$
The terminal sine is $\sin\left(30^\circ\right)=\frac{1}{2}$.
Therefore,
$$
\begin{aligned}
E&=\frac{8}{\left(\frac{1}{2}\right)}\\&=16.
\end{aligned}
$$

Let $E$ denote the given expression. Apply $\sin x\cos x=\frac{1}{2}\sin(2x)$ at $x=\frac{\pi}{16}$ in the denominator. Use $\sec x=1/\cos x$, then apply the double-angle identity again at $x=\frac{\pi}{8}$.
$$
\begin{aligned}
E&=\frac{2\,\sec\left(\frac{\pi}{8}\right)}{\sin\left(\frac{\pi}{8}\right)}\\&=\frac{2}{\sin\left(\frac{\pi}{8}\right)\cos\left(\frac{\pi}{8}\right)}\\&=\frac{4}{\sin\left(\frac{\pi}{4}\right)}
\end{aligned}
$$
The terminal sine is $\sin\left(\frac{\pi}{4}\right)=\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}$.
Rationalize the denominator by multiplying numerator and denominator by $\sqrt{2}$:
$$
\begin{aligned}
E&=\frac{4}{\left(\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}\right)}\\&=\frac{8}{\sqrt{2}}\\&=4 \sqrt{2}.
\end{aligned}
$$

Let $E$ denote the given expression. Apply $\sin x\cos x=\frac{1}{2}\sin(2x)$ at $x=37.5^\circ$. Dividing by secant multiplies by cosine because $1/\sec x=\cos x$. The resulting pair at $x=75^\circ$ permits a second application.
$$
\begin{aligned}
E&=\frac{\frac{3}{2}\,\sin\left(75^\circ\right)}{\sec\left(75^\circ\right)}\\&=\frac{3}{2}\,\sin\left(75^\circ\right)\cos\left(75^\circ\right)\\&=\frac{3}{4}\,\sin\left(150^\circ\right)
\end{aligned}
$$
The terminal sine is $\sin\left(150^\circ\right)=\frac{1}{2}$.
Therefore,
$$
\begin{aligned}
E&=\frac{3}{4}\,\left(\frac{1}{2}\right)\\&=\frac{3}{8}.
\end{aligned}
$$

Let $E$ denote the given expression. Apply $\sin x\cos x=\frac{1}{2}\sin(2x)$ at $x=\frac{7 \pi}{16}$. Dividing by secant multiplies by cosine because $1/\sec x=\cos x$. The resulting pair at $x=\frac{7 \pi}{8}$ permits a second application.
$$
\begin{aligned}
E&=\frac{\sin\left(\frac{7 \pi}{8}\right)}{\sec\left(\frac{7 \pi}{8}\right)}\\&=\sin\left(\frac{7 \pi}{8}\right)\cos\left(\frac{7 \pi}{8}\right)\\&=\frac{1}{2}\,\sin\left(\frac{7 \pi}{4}\right)
\end{aligned}
$$
The terminal sine is $\sin\left(\frac{7 \pi}{4}\right)=- \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}$.
Therefore,
$$
\begin{aligned}
E&=\frac{1}{2}\,\left(- \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}\right)\\&=- \frac{\sqrt{2}}{4}.
\end{aligned}
$$
